             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>The relation is a polynomial, so \(F\) and its partial derivatives are infinitely differentiable. Theorem 1.3 gives a continuously differentiable \(g\) on \(I\), and \(F_y \ne 0\) on \(I \times J\). If \(g\) is \(C^k\) (its first \(k\) derivatives exist and are continuous), then \(g' = -F_x(x, g(x))/F_y(x, g(x))\) is \(C^k\), so \(g\) is \(C^{k+1}\). By induction \(g\) is infinitely differentiable on \(I\), so its third derivative exists there. Assuming Assumption 1.2 and Assumption 2.1 of Lesson L06, the central difference of the root equals the slope plus \(\tfrac16 h^2\) times a third derivative of the root, so its truncation term at \(h = 10^{-5}\) is \(\tfrac16 h^2 = 1.7 \times 10^{-11}\) times that derivative. The rounding error of the computed roots is of order \(\varepsilon/h\) with \(\varepsilon \approx 2.2 \times 10^{-16}\), which is about \(2 \times 10^{-11}\) at this step. The printed \(3.9 \times 10^{-12}\) is below this scale, so the agreement is consistent with these two terms; the printed value does not measure either term. The agreement checks the formula at four points only. The theorem is the statement that the slope equals the formula for every \(x\) in \(I\), which four points cannot establish.</p>
