           <div class="problem" id="emp-3">
             <span class="problem-title">E3 · The residuals (Lab 1)</span>
             <p>Lab 1 prints residuals at the rounding level for each root. Explain why small residuals show that the computed \(y\) is close to a solution, and why they do not show that the root is the one that Theorem 1.3 provides.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>A residual \(|F(x, y)|\) at the rounding level means only that \((x, y)\) is nearly on the curve. Assuming Assumption 1.2 and Assumption 2.1 of Lesson L06, Theorem 1.3 gives \(g(x)\) as the unique zero of \(F(x, \cdot)\) in the interval \(J\) around \(y_0\), for \(x\) in an interval \(I\). The lab finds its root by Newton's method started at \(y = 1\), and its docstring says the iterates decrease to the root. Uniqueness on all of \(\R\) is a separate question: for \(x > 0\), \(F_y = 3y^2 + x > 0\) for every real \(y\), so \(F(x, \cdot)\) is strictly increasing and has exactly one real zero. Hence, for each \(x\) in \(I\), the Newton root equals \(g(x)\). The printed residuals do not show that the lab's \(x\)-values lie in \(I\), and the lab does not compute \(I\) or \(J\).</p>
